# UI/UX — motion

`context/instructions/ui-motion.md` is the reference for motion: what moves, how
long it may take, what it costs, and what it becomes when motion is unwelcome. It
is project-agnostic — it names the decisions, not the numbers. Read it with
`context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine), `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`
(the values), `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md` (the reduced-motion
principle) and `context/instructions/ui-components.md` (the states that move).

## When this applies

- Use it when a change adds, removes or changes motion — a transition, a feedback
  animation, a state change, an entrance. If the change does not move anything,
  this file does not apply: say so and stop.
- The project's own scale supplies every value. If the project has no motion
  tokens, this file still applies as decisions to follow, but never invent a scale
  as a side effect (see `ui-tokens.md`).

## Decide before you animate

Answer three questions, in this order. The third one is what makes the other two
bite.

1. **What kind of element is it?** A micro-feedback on a control, a card, a
   surface that takes focus, or a context switch. A heavier element can carry a
   longer movement than a lighter one, never a shorter one.
2. **How far does it travel?** Duration follows distance. The further something
   moves, the longer it needs — otherwise a long slide reads as a jump.
3. **How often does it play?** **The more often a motion plays, the shorter and
   plainer it must be.** A hover the user sees a thousand times a day earns a
   change of colour and nothing else; a first-run reveal that plays once can
   carry the full treatment.

A value you can measure is not a value to estimate: if the project already has a
duration or an easing, use it; if it has none, say so rather than inventing one.

## Duration and easing

- **Direction decides the curve.** Something entering **decelerates** — it moves
  quickly at first and settles. Something leaving **accelerates** — it eases in
  and leaves. Something that stays on screen moves smoothly at both ends. A loop
  must be seamless, so its two ends must match. **Linear is only for progress and
  continuous rotation** — a bar that fills, a spinner that turns.
- **Entering takes longer than leaving.** What appears is what the user reads;
  what disappears is cleanup. Give the exit a fraction of the entrance.
- **Direct response beats smooth.** A press, a hover and a release must answer
  before the user can notice the delay; the settling can take its time afterwards.

## What moves

- **Move and fade are cheap; anything else is not.** Transforming an element and
  changing its opacity are handled by the compositor and do not force the browser
  to recompute the page. Animating width, height, margin or padding does force a
  layout recalculation on every frame, so the page visibly stalls on a mid-range
  device: **move the element with a transform, never by animating its box.**
- An element scaled to zero disappears into a hole: keep a floor, and pair a
  scale with opacity rather than replacing one with the other.
- **Motion alone is not enough for an important change.** If a state change
  matters, it must be visible without relying on the animation alone — a
  transition that is skipped entirely must still leave a correct result.

## Staging

- **One hero per moment.** Give the surface a single focal movement; everything
  else supports it. Repeating the same entrance on every section is the failure
  mode this replaces.
- **Many elements means stagger, not a chorus.** When several things change at
  once, move them in an order a reader can follow — reading order, or outward
  from the focus — never all at the same instant.
- **A stagger has a budget.** The whole cascade must finish well under a second;
  anything longer makes the user wait for decoration. When a list outgrows the
  budget, animate fewer items rather than slowing the stagger.
- **Keep the motion vocabulary small.** A product whose every element moves
  differently reads as unfinished; name the three constants at the end of this
  file and hold to them.

## Motion and reduced motion

`ui-accessibility.md` owns the principle: `prefers-reduced-motion` must preserve
**hierarchy and feedback**, not kill every transition. This section is what that
principle substitutes:

- **Substitute, do not delete.** Remove the spatial part and keep the state
  change: a sliding entrance becomes a fade, a spring becomes a short ease, a
  parallax layer becomes a static position, a multi-step sequence collapses to one
  step, a continuous background loop becomes still.
- **Keep the transition that explains what changed.** If a change of state is
  carried by its animation, a reduced-motion user needs another way to read it —
  a visible state, a label, a position change — not silence.
- **Never auto-play a loop.** A movement that starts by itself and never stops is
  a distraction and an accessibility failure. Anything long enough to need a pause
  control needs one.
- **Avoid motion that provokes.** Large scale changes, full-screen movement,
  spinning elements and stacked parallax can cause real discomfort. When one is
  genuinely needed, provide an alternative.

## Symptom to fix

| It feels | Likely cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Robotic | no deceleration, or a perfectly straight path | decelerate the arrival; let the path bend slightly |
| Slow | the movement is longer than the element deserves | shorten toward the project's shortest tier, keep only the decelerating half |
| Cheap or flat | only the moved thing moves | let what supports it answer too — the shadow, the icon, the neighbour |
| Distracting | too much moving at once | apply the one-hero rule, shorten the stagger budget |
| Jarring | no settling beat | add a fraction of a second of stillness and let the eye land |
| No personality | every screen picks its own timing | take the three constants below instead of choosing per screen |
| Inconsistent | the same kind of change moves differently in two places | take the values from the project's own scale, not from the screen |

## The product's motion identity

Name three things once, then hold to them:

- **one signature easing** used by most of the product's motion;
- **a small duration palette** — a quick, a normal and a slow tier — rather than a
  value chosen per screen;
- **one way of arriving** for repeated elements.

Record them where the project keeps conventions, not in this file.

## Boundary

- The doctrine is `context/instructions/ui.md`; the values come from
  `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`.
- The reduced-motion principle and the review checklist are
  `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md`; this file supplies its
  substitutions.
- States, focus and overlays are `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
- How a framework declares transitions, and honours reduced motion without
  flattening it, is `context/instructions/ui-adapters.md`.
- Confirming the motion actually settled needs a real render:
  `context/instructions/browser.md`.
